  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 239 AM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThursday, our region is forecast to be in a mostly southwesterly \nupper-level flow with a developing trough in the Pacific Northwest \n(PNW). The track and evolution of this trough will determine \nprecipitation chances heading into the weekend and early next week.\n\nHigh temperatures Thursday and Friday are forecast to be pleasant, \nwith most of the county warning area (CWA) reaching the upper 60s to \n70s. Friday is when we start to see a potential pattern change from \nthe aforementioned trough in the PNW. \n\nCurrently, the trough is forecast to become more of a cut off low \nsometime late Friday/early Saturday. This would favor more \nwidespread precipitation chances as it would generally keep our \nregion in a southwest upper-level flow with various embedded \nshortwaves being ejected across the area Friday through Monday. \nCurrent Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) are around 20-40% Friday \nthrough Monday. Precipitation is expected to generally be showers. \nInstability is overall weak Friday with modest lapse rates and \nspotty CAPE values of <500 J/kg, so a weak thunderstorm or two isn't \nout of the question if it is able to tap into the weak instability \nin the environment. \n\nIf the trough digs farther south instead of becoming a cutoff low, a \nmore active weather pattern could develop due to stronger synoptic \nforcing and greater moisture availability. \n\nOutside of precipitation chances for the weekend, temperatures are \nexpected to be mild with high temperatures forecast in the 60s. \nMonday has the potential to be a windy day with gusts up to 50 mph \npossible for eastern Colorado. This will also depend on how our \nsystem for the weekend pans out, but something to keep an eye\non.",
